About this course
======
This course is a selective one for sophomore students. I prepared the lectures based on the textbook Operations Management: Creating Value Along the Supply Chain, written by Russell, R. S., & Taylor, B. W. (John Wiley & Sons, 2019). This course introduces operations management as a central function through which companies create value and gain competitive advantage. Students will be guided to learn how a company coordinates its internal processes with suppliers and customers to produce products and services that provide value, and to grasp essential analytical methods and tools used in operational decision-making. The course emphasizes the strategic implications of operating decisions, examining how firms deploy the basic functions of operations management (such as process design, capacity planning, inventory management, quality control, and supply chain coordination) more effectively than their rivals to build a competitive edge. Students are placed in the role of operations decision makers and asked to address questions central to creating value along the supply chain. The initial focus is on understanding how operations fit into a company's overall strategic design, after which we study the specific functions and processes through which value is created and delivered to customers. Throughout the course, numerous real-world examples drawn from the "Along the Supply Chain" cases in the textbook are used to illustrate how successful companies have gained a competitive edge through operations. Performance will be assessed by the following parts and in corresponding weights: participation accounts for 15%, class assignments account for 40%, and the final exam accounts for 45%.
